[20.125, "o", "\u001b[2m🔍 breaking down command...\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[21.025, "o", "\r\n"]
[21.175, "o", "Let's break down the command \u001b[0;32mtar -xzvf backup.tar.gz -C /tmp\u001b[0m for a beginner:\r\n\r\n- \u001b[0;32mtar\u001b[0m: This is the command-line utility used to create, extract, and manipulate archive files (also known as tarballs). It stands for \"tape archive.\"\r\n\r\n- \u001b[0;32m-x\u001b[0m: This option tells \u001b[0;32mtar\u001b[0m to **extract** files from the archive. It stands for \"extract.\"\r\n\r\n- \u001b[0;32m-z\u001b[0m: This option tells \u001b[0;32mtar\u001b[0m to **filter the archive through gzip**. It means the archive is compressed with gzip, and \u001b[0;32mtar\u001b[0m will automatically decompress it.\r\n\r\n- \u001b[0;32m-v\u001b[0m: This option stands for **verbose**. It makes \u001b[0;32mtar\u001b[0m print the names of the files as they are extracted, so you can see what's happening.\r\n\r\n- \u001b[0;32m-f\u001b[0m: This option is used to specify the **filename** of the archive. It must be followed by the name of the archive file.\r\n\r\n- \u001b[0;32mbackup.tar.gz\u001b[0m: This is the **name of the archive file** being extracted. The \u001b[0;32m.tar.gz\u001b[0m extension indicates it's a tarball compressed with gzip.\r\n\r\n- \u001b[0;32m-C /tmp\u001b[0m: This option tells \u001b[0;32mtar\u001b[0m to **change to the specified directory** before extracting the files. In this case, it changes to the \u001b[0;32m/tmp\u001b[0m directory, and all files will be extracted there.\r\n\r\n**Summary:** The command \u001b[0;32mtar -xzvf backup.tar.gz -C /tmp\u001b[0m extracts the contents of the compressed archive file \u001b[0;32mbackup.tar.gz\u001b[0m into the \u001b[0;32m/tmp\u001b[0m directory, showing the progress of the extraction."]
